WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.140 --> 00:00:01.320
Use a bounded example.

2
00:00:01.860 --> 00:00:06.060
Six twelve-kilowatt units give seventy-two kilowatts of installed nameplate capacity.

3
00:00:06.520 --> 00:00:10.460
If the required case has one unit unavailable, five units remain, giving

4
00:00:10.540 --> 00:00:13.460
sixty kilowatts of supply capacity before other constraints.

5
00:00:14.000 --> 00:00:17.720
Now add a downstream path qualified for fifty-five kilowatts under the stated

6
00:00:17.780 --> 00:00:18.300
conditions.

7
00:00:18.740 --> 00:00:21.820
That path is tighter than the remaining supply total, so the example

8
00:00:21.860 --> 00:00:26.480
supports at most fifty-five kilowatts at that boundary, before any additional operating

9
00:00:26.540 --> 00:00:27.380
or reserve margin.

10
00:00:28.020 --> 00:00:31.880
Seventy-two, sixty and fifty-five answer three different questions.

11
00:00:32.259 --> 00:00:34.580
The animation applies these constraints in sequence.

12
00:00:34.960 --> 00:00:38.120
It doesn't imply that one failed unit is equivalent to losing any

13
00:00:38.160 --> 00:00:39.140
possible input feed.

14
00:00:39.620 --> 00:00:42.840
For a real design, the required loss might remove a shared path

15
00:00:43.020 --> 00:00:44.180
or impose another limit.

16
00:00:44.580 --> 00:00:47.180
Recalculate the actual topology under that case.

17
00:00:47.740 --> 00:00:51.560
Usable capacity is the supported result after the relevant constraints are applied,

18
00:00:51.880 --> 00:00:54.520
not a nameplate sum with a redundancy label attached.

